A||||20||dman||Topless Fiero (long post)||||6||
Z||000000||dman||07-18-2000||08:39 PM||||Hello everyone. A recent thread in the "General Fiero Chat" section on kit cars prompted me to write about my thoughts on making one of my fiero cars a convertible.<P>Actually the car that I'm thinking about making a convertible is a Testarossa rebody, but since it is based on a Fiero, the majority of the work involved would be near identical for most fiero based cars.<P>I'd like to conduct a discussion on the following three points.<P>1) Frame strengthening. What different techniques are there. What are the pros and cons of each, and which one would you settle on.<P>2) Convertible top construction. Targa top? Semi-hard top? Complete removeable hardtop (like some mercedes benz cars)?<P>3) Kit makers and availability (for those who don't wish to roll their own).<P>4) Do it yourself plans. For those who would rather do it all by themself.<P>While I love to read about outrageous and over-the-top, automobile modifications, I'd like to maintain the discussion towards realistic and achievable strategies that an average wrench-turner with a well equipped garage can use.<P>For myself, I prefer to do the conversion without the use of a kit. I've heard that Bill Welch in Florida makes a kit. I spoke to Phil Huff at FOCOA and he tells me that he'll have some ready by September.<P>I think I'd like to go with a semi-hard top solution. The roof panel would be of solid construction and would be stored behind the seats or perhaps in the front trunk; I will probably use the original fiero roof as a basis for it. The B pillar would be retained so that (in conjuction with the roof) the original door rubber could be used for proper weatherproofing. The B pillar would be hinged so it could fold backwards (towards rear of the car).<P>I have a few other thoughts on this but this post would just get even longer. I also wonder though how many people here on the forum would be interested on this subject. So for now let me point you to that original thread in the "General Fiero Chat" section so you can read a little more about what I'm planning.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002966.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002966.html</A> <P>I appreciate any and all useful comments you may have. Thanks!!!<P><BR>  ||||reg||6
Z||000001||rockcrawl||07-18-2000||09:26 PM||||dman,<BR> Wow, It looks like we are in the same boat! I'm also working on a Rossa Spyder. I also prefer to do as much as I can myself, I find that is the only way to be assured the quality will meet my standards, and the budget will not be exceeded.<BR> I have taken a break from the project, so I have plenty of time to decide on a top. My original thought was to simply have no top at all, just a boot with nothing under it. Most recently my thoughts have turned to finding a small convertible in the salvage yard, and reworking the frame to fit the Fiero. I can get a sunbird top for next to nothing. Unfortunately I have never owned a convertible of any kind, and I am clueless as to how they work. <BR> My chassis is extended 7 inches, and everything behind the seats is custom made to fit a cadillac engine and coilover suspension. I cut the sheet metal rocker panels off, and welded 2x4" steel tubing to the "frame" that was exposed. The top of the tubing is at the same height as the original rocker, and it extends down to the bottom of the pinch weld. Smaller tubing (2x3) could easily be used and hidden inside the Fiero rockers. The tubing starts as far up under the door hinges as I could get it, and hangs out the back because again, my chassis is stretched. I also ran the tubing up the back at the firewall angle, and tied into the upper rail. Even with all this (heavy) steel, it was still very flimsy. The front of the car is fine, but it seems to want to bend in half where the center hump meets the firewall. So I ran some 1x1 tubing on top of the hump, and up the inside of the firewall to the heavy cross brace at the bottom of the rear window (inside the console), and then triangulated the two. This stiffened things up pretty well, but I'm still not completely satisfied. I'll keep adding more weight until I am.<BR> Do you have any pictures of you car? If so, I'd love to see them. Maybe it'll get me moving on mine! I've been spending most of my time on my other Fieros.<P>Later,<BR>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT||||reg||10
Z||000002||80's BOY||07-19-2000||01:08 AM||||for the Texas Targa project (still underway) i used a rollbar style reinforcement with pipes from foot to rear on the interior. these are also powdercoated to make them look nice. i tossed around the x frame idea, but the fuel pump question could never be answered. you know, what happens if it goes out and i've welded this thing under my car. i think it was Gary's Custom Exotics in Florida that said he has one with a removeable center piece. i may be mistaken. another idea was to remove the stock cooling pipes and replace them with larger, stronger pipes thus making the car more stable while giving it a greater coolant capacity for hotter weather. these would be welded in of course. my car has a very stable frame thanks to the roll bar method i have used. i still check my measurements often just in case. as for the targa top................<P>that's a different story, but it's really quite easy!! GM did all the hard part for you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fret Naught||07-19-2000||01:44 AM||||I up for it, I won't start my project until next summer, I got a bit too much on my plate right now, but information gathering is a big part. I like the idea of a detachable top, but 80'S Boy's project sound very interesting, hopefully we will see some pic's (hint hint) I think the roll bar and targa top will achive the look I am most interested in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spyder||07-19-2000||12:19 PM||||If you mean doing something like this????<BR>It can’t be done…..J/K.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/atesta1.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/atesta2.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/atesta3.gif"> <BR>Here is the page where it came from. <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/customkar/Ferrari_kitcar/Page_3x.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/customkar/Ferrari_kitcar/Page_3x.html</A> <BR>Now, I have had no, ZERO, dealings with these guys.<BR>I’m not sure if these are the same people, but I had heard one negative incident.<P>---------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/SpydersFieroa.gif">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/SpydersFiero.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spyder||07-19-2000||01:59 PM||||I have the X frame, 1"x2" box tubing 3/8" thick. The frame is an x that is boxed out.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/xframe.JPG"> <P>The top header on my car is from a second generation Sunbird with modified bows and new custom frame.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/topd.jpg"> <P>I have more pics on my site in the My f355 section. <A HREF="http://www.MarkAlexanderMiller.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.MarkAlexanderMiller.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||dman||07-20-2000||03:21 PM||||Hello everyone. I'm really quite excited that there are others here on the Forum who are also interested in dicussing convertible converesions.<P>rockcrawl, welcome to the forum. BTW, I came across your web page a few months back and I think your Countach is awesome! A longitudinally mounted V8 Countach, full cage, and so many more goodies, very impressive!!!<P>You may wish to know that your "bio" refers to an invalid homepage address, It says "click.to" instead of "clik.to".<P>Thanks for the description of your frame strenghening, I'm gonna have to print it out so I can read it while looking over my car to properly visualize what you've done. You've done a lot of work so your comment that you're still not completely satisfied leaves me to wonder if a stretched chassis requires more reinforcement than a plain Fiero. Hmm...I'll try to talk to my ex-kitcar-maker friend and find out what he thinks. If you have any pics of your frame strengthening I'd love to see them. When I work on my cars this weekend I'll take some pics of the Testarossa for you, I'll also try to take some pics of the convertible top frame to show you what I'm thinking of for the Testarossa top.<P>80's Boy, thanks for the update on the "Texas Targa Project". I like the idea of the rollbar, however I don't know what you mean when you describe the pipes as going from "foot to rear". And yeah, a completely welded in X-frame sounds a little bit scary when it's time to replace the fuel pump. My notchback convertible has more like a box frame so that isn't an issue.<P>Please elaborate on what you mean when you say that "GM did all the hard part for you" when it comes to the targa top. I'm quite interested!<P>As usual, pics would be very helpful and instructive. Plus, its just so cool to look at awesome fieros!<P>Fred Naught, though you may not start your project until next summer I'm glad you're here to share some info as well as comments. Though I'm most interested in the mechanics of making my Testarossa a convertible, I'd love to be able to apply the knowledge and experience learned to make one of my other fieros a Targa top car.<P>Spyder, thanks for the pic and the link! I'd already come across that site but wasn't at all impressed at the pics. I figured if they looked that bad, then what would the quality of their cars and kits be like? (I hope much better)<P>BTW, I've been following the progress of your 355 project for a few months now. It was funny because for at least a month or so I didn't know that you (Spyder on the forum) were the same person as the Mark that was documenting the build of his 355 spyder.<P>Way to go Spyder! Your project looks like it is going great and I look forward to your updates every week. I think it is great that you are documenting your build.<P>Ah, once again, a request for pics. If you could, when you get your camera back, could you take some pics of the convertible top system you are using? I'm quite interested to know how the back part folds down and how much of the Sunbird(Sunfire?) top was retained.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||80's BOY||07-20-2000||04:33 PM||||he dman, i guess that didn't make much sense, did it. what i mean is the roll bars are 2 inch pipe which have a metal foot pad welded on the top and the bottom of them. remove all carpet and weld in the bar at an angle almost parallell to the door arm rest on the door. what you have is a rool bar which is safe, but still easy enough to crawl over to get in.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||07-20-2000||08:34 PM||||dman,<BR> I'll take some more pics of my wannabe Rossa and post them on my site tommorow. I'll post the links here also. That's if I can find it under all the stuff that has been piling up on it for the last few months! <P>I think I'm going to get the Sunbird top frame and hardware and make it fit. A convertible 'kit' sounds like too much money. Does anyone have any prices for kits?<P>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||12
Z||000009||dman||07-20-2000||10:23 PM||||I've found the following webpage but I've never spoken to Bill about his kit. I think a forum member here has tried to obtain the kit but I don't know how things worked out. Here's the URL:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/8487/billconv.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/8487/billconv.htm</A> <P>I can't seem to connect to it right now but it worked last week.<P>I spoke to Phil Huff of FOCOA and he said after the nationals, perhaps around September he will have a few available. Price for the FOCOA kit? Don't know, give Phil a call. He's fun to talk to. Here's the webpage, the number is down on the bottom:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroowners.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroowners.com/</A> <P><BR>The Big Fiero Parts book lists a company called Fiero Conversions (I think in Ontario, Canada) as having a convertible top. I don't know anything about it or the price but maybe someone else knows.<P>Yeah, a sunbird(sunfire?) top may be a good idea. But for your TestaRossa check out the following URL. I've emailed the guys there and they seem like they can help. I'm waiting to get some more info from them. They are using a semi-hardtop that I think I'd like to give a try. In one of the photos you can see how they place the top behind the seats for storage. It would probably be a lot easier then trying to fab a frame from scratch, ah heck, it may even be easier (or at least just as hard) as trying to adapt a sunbird frame. Either way, please let us know what you find out about these Sunbird tops. Sounds interesting.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.512tr.web.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.512tr.web.com/</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||07-22-2000||09:12 AM||||would something like the mini truckers do work? that deal where they take the top off whole like a cap that they can put back on? i just got another fiero and would someday (in a far distant future) like to have it be a convertible.<P>------------------<BR><P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy||||reg||1
Z||000011||80's BOY||07-22-2000||12:11 PM||||there was a company called AIM industries in the late 80's, early 90's that had a universal targa kit for mini trucks and blazers. i tried to find one last year and was told they no longer make them. the biggest problem is getting the seals to attach to the targa piece you are taking off. this is somewhat easy on the Fiero since the windows already roll up OVER the seals. just keep the upper door seal pieces, contersink the rivets when attaching to the top and glass over them for a smooth look. fiberglass is much easier than metal roofs. my biggest problem so far has been that my windshield frame flexed to the right  1/2" and so i have to trim the targa piece accordingly.||||reg||4
Z||000012||rockcrawl||07-22-2000||07:24 PM||||Hey dman,<BR> Thanks for that 512TR link! that looks like something I can do, and it looks great. I like the idea of using a hoop cut from the original Fiero roof, and a targa section. I'll probably do something like that, and use the haeder panel and latches from a sunbird. I would like to have a one piece folding top though. Too bad I already scrapped the fiero roof! <P>Bills kits look great also, but are too rich for my blood.<P>Philphine reminded me of an idea I had a few years back. I had plans of cutting the roof off my 86 coupe and using a mini truck kit for the hardware, latches, seals etc.. I never got around to it. The removeable hardtop and targa kits are still readily available in many different forms, for lots of different trucks. For a targa or hardtop I think thats the way I'd go. The kits were cheap!<P>I'm going to the salvage yard this week to check out the sunbird top (late 80s, early 90s?)that I saw there last week. I'm also going to make an offer on a burned SE for parts (aero  panels, headlight assys, wheels, roof panel). <P>Later,<BR>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||10
Z||000013||rockcrawl||07-22-2000||08:34 PM||||Hi,<BR> I've got some more pictures of my frame stiffening on my site, follow the link <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/ferrari.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/ferrari.html</A> <P>Jon<P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||2
Z||000014||Spyder||07-23-2000||02:08 PM||||The car to look for is any 1988 to 1994 Sunbird or Cavalier Convertible.<BR>If any one needs close ups or any specifics just give me an email.<BR> <BR>rockcrawl:<BR>I feel right at home looking at your pics.<P>dman:<BR>Did you get the email that I sent you?<BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Philphine||07-23-2000||08:21 PM||||i think what i was talking about before is a carson top. <P>rock, that looked like a whole lot more bracing than an x frame. did you think it was nessesary because of your added rear sub frame or just personal insurance on your part?<P>the sun bird top. just the front bow or do you get the whole thing? do you get a shop to rework it? rebend or cut it?<P>the x frame. would it be possible to start with a rectangle the size of the fuel tank and have arms kind of branch off of it to the frame? that might let you drop the tank when it's needed. or would that be weaker structuraly? maybe with a bolt in x within the rectangle?<P>i'll probaly never do this but i can collect the parts (the car itself, sunbird top,etc.) and hope for a chance in the future. <P>on a side note rock. why did you choose your engine? it was talked about some but nothing really conclusive was said. i guess with your custom subframe what you've done can't be compleatly applied to a stock frame fiero but any info helps since it was decided if nothing else it should be an easy engine to afford and find.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Spyder||07-24-2000||11:34 AM||||When you use the Sunbird top you mainly use the front header and hardware.  On mine one of the sunbird bows is modified to be about two inches wider. And the rest is custom made.<BR>Don‘t forget to get the two parts that the top locks into on the Sunbird windshield.<BR>Also I will be adding another bow in the center of mine.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spyder||07-24-2000||12:05 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Toppic.JPG"> <BR>Here is another way it can be done. <BR>The side windows would seal better.||||reg||1
Z||000018||dman||07-24-2000||11:13 PM||||Hello everyone. Hope you guys had a good weekend.<P>Philphine, that sounds like a good idea. Some Mercedes-Benz cars were like that too. You could have a winch in your garage to lift it on and off (and of course store it). <P>80's boy, I didn't consider that the latches/fittings/etc could perhaps be obtained from a mini-truck targa kit. Thanks 80's boy! I hope to find some time to look into this further, unless of course one of you have found a place online that describes one of these kits.<P>rockcrawl, thanks for the pics!!! Wow, that 4.9 sounds sweet. I had considered that same engine for transplanting into my Ferrari 328. I had even gone down to Chris West's shop to talk to him about it. I would have done most of the work but probably would have needed Chris' help on a custom flywheel and perhaps a few other incidentals.<P>I'd say you've done a massive amount of framework (to adapt the 4.9) as well as strengthen due to chopping the top. Your pics show a lot of detail. Good stuff!!! The box tubing where the rockers are (or used to be) sound like a good idea. Probably ideal for a rebody, but perhaps not that applicable for a regular fiero. I've noticed though that you don't seem to have a cross member that ties (what used to be) the B pillar on the drivers side to the passenger side. I think if you added a small 1x3 to tie them together that you may get some extra strengthening. You could lay it right on top of the rear firewall. I just looked at a Ferrari F40 convertible this weekend and noticed that it had such a cross member. I only drove it a couple of blocks (the engine was near death) but it felt pretty solid.<P>Spyder, thanks for the info on the Sunbird model years! And thanks too for the pics you sent via email.<P>Philphine, do you mean "Corson" top instead of "Carson" top? I know Corson used to make Ferrari like kit cars as well as a top, but I can't seem to find the company. The phone number I found on a webpage no longer seems to work. They made some pretty complete and nice looking kits.<P>The Ferrari F40 I looked at this weekend, as well as my convertible Fiero notchback both have a box frame strengthener underneath that does not have the X cross pieces. This allows me to drop the fuel tank when it becomes necessary to replace the fuel pump. I don't know if it is any weaker without an X design but I've actually seen this exact setup on 3 convertible cars now. I've heard on the internet that some X frames were available where the X part was bolted so that gas tank removal was still possible.<P><BR>And finally to me, I didn't have much time to work on my cars this weekend so I don't have any pics to share. Sorry!!! But I did get the convertible running again. I'll need to replace the front left caliper and some other minor work but with any luck I'll be able to cruise her around within a month or two.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||rockcrawl||07-25-2000||06:39 PM||||Hi everyone,<BR> I just got back from the junk yard. I worked a deal on an '86 SE, burned, but lots of good parts. I also checked out the Sunbird top. He wants $75 for everything minus actuators. It looks like it will work great with some minor mods. I'll definately use the header panel and latches, and some of the bows (modified). I will probably cut a hoop section from the fiero roof where the weatherstrip for the rear edge of the window goes, and use it as part of the framework. It's kinda hard to describe the pictures I have in my mind.<P>Philphine,<BR> A lot of the framework I did on my car was strictly due to the lengthening of the wheelbase, and the engine/cradle/suspension mods. The only part that stiffens the frame for the roof being cut off is the tubing that replaces the sheetmetal rockers, and the small bracing in the console area. <P>I chose the 4.9 Cad engine because it's cheap, powerfull, light (same weight as a 2.8) and it will fit in a fiero easier than  a SBC or Northstar. It's like a poor-man's Northstar! I paid $350 for my engine and it came with a Cadillac. Check this link for more info <A HREF="http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/index.html</A> <P>Spyder, Is the X-frame the only stiffening that was added to your Chassis? I can't believe that would be enough. It prevents twisting, but doesn't do much to keep the car from folding in half at the bottom of the firewall area, where mine seemed to be very flexible. I think I will add a removeable (bolt on) X to the bottom of mine to help prevent twisting though.<P>Dman, I didn't add any support across the top of the firewall because the existing sheetmetal is layered, and heavy guage. I didn't think it was necessary, although I did consider doing it just to tie everything together a little better. It's tough to combine heavy wall tubing and sheetmetal "space frame" and get a good sturdy connection. Like I said, I'm not nearly done yet.<P>Pisa sells a targa top kit that is manufactured by Corson Motorsports. It includes a "metal undercarriage frame support" and everything else you need for $1999. No picture of the frame. They also sell a Corson V8 conversion, maybe we could get ahold of PI$A for info on Corson products.<P>A few years ago at Carlisle I saw a guy with a convertible Fastback. From what I remember, the only strengthening done was replacing the coolant tubes with heavy square tubing with the round ends welded on and coolant running through them. <P>Tired of typing,<BR>Jon<P><P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000020||Spyder||07-27-2000||04:34 PM||||rockcrawl <BR>On the flexing up on the X frame, It doesn’t have much.  The only time I have noticed any was when I had the car on jack stands,  the stands placed on the very front where I had extended the front rails and in the back where the frame rails end. This made the doors rub a little. If I notice any more flexing latter, I’ll add a bar up and down in front of the rear wheel. But I am about to add the subwoofer enclosure which runs all the way across  the car from side frame to side frame, from floor board to the top cross beam. So when this is bonded in, it should help there also. <BR>||||reg||1
